## Known limitations / future work

- **Cross-island preview on the overlay** — both NavMesh legs currently concatenate into `navmesh_points`; the projector renders them as one polyline. Acceptable for single-island production. Upgrade the projector to accept a list of NavMesh legs when a second arena goes live.
- **NavMesh leg quality on cross-island** — only single-mesh validated straightening per leg. If a future cross-island route produces a visibly zigzag NavMesh leg, extend `_navmesh_leg` to also apply Laplacian smoothing.
- **OSM graph selection** — one graph (Gothenburg) shared across calls. If new cities are added, expand `OsmGraphService` to select the relevant graph per island pair.
- **Failure recovery** — any failed leg aborts the whole route. A fallback policy ("if the NavMesh leg to the entrance fails, try the next-nearest entrance") could be added once the multi-island dataset exposes which entrances actually reach which parts of an island.
- **`compute_routes_to_exits` (one-shot bulk measure)** — implemented in `route_measure/exits.py`; primary path still calls `compute_route_measure` (`route_measure/compute_single.py`) directly because it iterates over many endpoints in one request and benefits from a single-call API. **Shortcut-aware fallback**: when the dev "Use shortcuts" toggle is on (`enable_shortcuts=True`), every entry that returns `success=False` is retried via `RouteComposer.compose(enable_shortcuts=True)` so destinations behind stairs become discoverable in wheelchair mode through an elevator hop. See `shortcuts.mdc` § "POI / quiet / exit visibility (shortcut-aware fallback)" for caveats. If the exits widget needs OSM-aware measures in the future, fold it through `compose_with_measure`.

